---
/**
 * About page.
 *
 * Deliberately concrete: what the tool does, which gazette the dates come from,
 * and what the solver will and will not do. Every figure quoted here is one the
 * solver produces for 2026 — nine free long weekends, 17 gazetted national
 * holidays, 22 four-day breaks for a single leave day. The FAQ test on the
 * homepage pins those same numbers, so if the holiday data changes this page has
 * to be revisited too. There is a note about that in the verification script.
 */
import DocPage from "../layouts/DocPage.astro";
import { COUNTRIES } from "../data/countries";

/** Counted from the shipped data, so the prose below cannot outlive it. */
const COUNTRY_COUNT = COUNTRIES.length;

const TITLE = "About – How The Long Weekends Works | The Long Weekends";
const DESCRIPTION =
  `How The Long Weekends plans your 2026 and 2027 leave across ${COUNTRY_COUNT} countries: where the holiday dates come from, how the solver picks the cheapest long break, and what it will not do.`;
---

<DocPage
  title={TITLE}
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  path="/about"
  eyebrow="About"
  heading="Why this site exists"
  lede="Public holidays are fixed. Your leave balance is not. This tool lines the two up and tells you the cheapest way to buy a long break."
  updated="2026-08-24"
>
  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p>
    Most holiday calendars show you a grid of dates and stop there. The useful
    question is not <em>when is Diwali</em> — it is <strong>which leave days are
    worth spending</strong>, and that depends on which weekday each holiday lands
    on.
  </p>
  <p>
    2026 has <strong>nine long weekends that cost no leave at all</strong>, because
    the holiday falls on a Friday or a Monday. Spend a single leave day and <strong>22 four-day breaks</strong> open up across 13 different festivals. The
    calculator finds these for you and sorts them by how soon they arrive, so you
    can book the next one rather than reading a list of dates that have passed.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the dates come from</h2>
  <p>
    India and the United States are transcribed from official notifications, not
    aggregated from other calendar sites:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      the <strong>Department of Personnel and Training (DoPT)</strong> gazetted
      holiday list for central government offices
    </li>
    <li>
      the <strong>Reserve Bank of India</strong> list under section 25 of the
      Negotiable Instruments Act, which is what banks actually close for
    </li>
    <li>
      the <strong>U.S. Office of Personnel Management</strong> federal holiday
      schedule, which states each holiday on the date it is actually observed
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The other {COUNTRY_COUNT - 2} countries are compiled from Google’s published
    public-holiday calendars and cross-checked against Nager.Date, with every
    disagreement between the two reviewed by hand before it ships. The compiler
    lives in the repository rather than in someone’s notes, so each list can be
    regenerated the moment a government publishes its next year.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every list is the <strong>nationwide</strong> one. This is a country-level
    planner: it does not carry sub-national calendars for anywhere, including
    India. Where a country’s holidays genuinely vary from one part of it to
    another, a day is included when it is observed across most of the country
    and left out when it is not — so the calendar you get is the one a national
    employer would publish, and your own office list is the thing that will
    differ. That is what the <a href="/company-optimizer">office holiday
    optimizer</a> is for.
  </p>
  <p>
    Dates set by lunar observation — Eid, Diwali, Vesak, Nyepi — can move by a
    day even after a government publishes them, and a few national notifications
    land only weeks ahead. Indonesia’s 2027 is an estimate for exactly that
    reason: the decree that fixes it is not out yet. If you spot a date that has
    moved, <a href="/contact">tell us</a> — it is the single most useful thing
    you can report. The <a href="/countries">country list</a> says which
    calendars carry sighting-dependent dates.
  </p>

  <h2>How the maths works</h2>
  <p>
    For a given number of leave days, the solver walks the calendar looking for
    runs of consecutive days off, treating your weekend, the public holidays for
    your region, and the leave you are willing to spend as one continuous block.
    Two rules keep the results honest:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Every plan must contain a real holiday.</strong> Taking Friday and
      Monday off around an ordinary weekend is not a long weekend, it is four days
      of leave.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A plan must return at least 1.3 days off per leave day spent.</strong> Below that you are not bridging a gap, you are just taking leave, and the
      tool has nothing to add.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Results are then ranked by <strong>how soon they start</strong>, not by how
    long they are. A spectacular 18-day break next March is no use to someone
    deciding what to do in three weeks.
  </p>

  <h2>Weekends are not the same everywhere</h2>
  <p>
    Which days a country rests on changes every answer on the page, so the solver
    uses each country’s own rather than assuming Saturday and Sunday. Israel works
    Sunday to Thursday and rests Friday and Saturday; Egypt does the same. On
    those calendars a holiday landing on a Sunday is a real day off rather than
    one the weekend had already swallowed — get that wrong and every result is
    shifted by two days.
  </p>
  <p>
    Separately, a large share of Indian workplaces run Monday to Saturday. The
    work-week switch in the header re-solves the whole calendar for a six-day
    week rather than adjusting the display: a Saturday holiday becomes a real day
    off, and Friday holidays stop producing three-day weekends.
  </p>

  <h2>Your office holiday list</h2>
  <p>
    Most companies publish their own list, which overlaps the gazette but is never
    identical. The <a href="/company-optimizer">office holiday optimizer</a> takes
    that list — pasted as text, or as a PDF or image — reads the dates out of it,
    and runs the same solver against your actual holidays.
  </p>
  <p>
    The parsing happens <strong>in your browser</strong>. Your company's holiday
    list is not uploaded anywhere, which is both a privacy property and the reason
    the feature works without an account. There is more detail in the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What it does not do</h2>
  <p>
    Worth knowing before you rely on it:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      It covers <strong>2026 and 2027</strong>, plus the first days of January
      2028 so that a New Year bridge can be calculated. It stops there.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It works at country level only.</strong> Every calendar here is
      the national one, so wherever a country’s holidays vary internally you
      may get days this does not know about — and it may show one you do not
      get.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>China’s make-up workdays are not modelled.</strong> Golden Week is
      paid for by turning a neighbouring weekend into working days, and that
      swap is published separately each autumn.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Restricted or optional holidays</strong> are not modelled. Those vary
      per employer and are usually the interesting ones, which is what the office
      optimizer is for.
    </li>
    <li>
      It does not know your leave balance, your team's blackout dates, or whether
      your manager will approve anything.
    </li>
    <li>
      It is not a booking site and has no commercial relationship with any airline
      or hotel.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who runs it</h2>
  <p>
    An independent project, built and maintained by one person in India — which
    is why India is the calendar with the most care in it, and why corrections
    for the other {COUNTRY_COUNT - 1} are especially welcome. There is no company
    behind it, no sign-up, no newsletter and no advertising. If it is
    useful, the best thanks is to send a correction when a date is wrong — the
    address is on the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.
  </p>
</DocPage>
